# v6-benjamini-hochberg

Federated Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate correction across multiple
organizations without sharing raw data.

This algorithm is designed to be run with the [vantage6](https://vantage6.ai)
infrastructure for distributed analysis and learning.

## What it does

When you test many columns at once — one hypothesis per column — the chance of
at least one false positive grows with the number of tests. The
Benjamini-Hochberg procedure controls the *false discovery rate*: the expected
proportion of rejected hypotheses that are actually true.

The algorithm exposes two entry points.

### `central` — correct p-values you already have

Takes a family of p-values produced by a previous federated analysis (for
example a run of `v6-kruskal-wallis-py`, `v6-mann-whitney-u-test-py`,
`v6-t-test-py` or `v6-anova-py` over several columns) and returns adjusted
p-values and rejection decisions. It creates no subtasks, contacts no node and
opens no database.

```python
{
  "method": "central",
  "kwargs": {
    "p_values": {"age": 0.0001, "Weight": 0.0031, "Height": 0.0402, "bmi": 0.21},
    "alpha": 0.05
  }
}
```

Returns `n_hypotheses`, `n_tested`, `n_rejected` and, per hypothesis,
`p_value`, `adjusted_p_value`, `reject`, `rank` and `critical_value`.
p-values that are `null` or `NaN` are passed through with `tested: false` and
are excluded from the family size `m`.

### `central_federated` — test the columns and correct in one run

Runs a rank-based test on every selected column across the participating
organizations, then applies the correction to the resulting family of p-values.

```python
{
  "method": "central_federated",
  "kwargs": {
    "organizations_to_include": [1, 2, 3],
    "group_col": "Group",
    "columns": ["age", "Weight", "Height", "bmi", "sbp"],
    "test": "kruskal-wallis",
    "alpha": 0.05
  }
}
```

`test` is either `"kruskal-wallis"` (two or more groups) or `"mann-whitney"`
(exactly two groups).

## How raw data is kept at the node

A rank-based test needs a *global* ordering of observations, which is normally
obtained by sending every value to the central server. This algorithm never does
that. Instead it runs three rounds, and each round transmits only aggregates.

**Round 1 — `partial_moments`.** Each node returns, per column, three numbers:
the record count, the sum, and the sum of squares. The central server pools these
into a global mean and standard deviation and uses them to lay out a shared
reporting grid spanning `mean ± 4 sd`. No minimum, maximum, quantile or
individual value is involved, so the grid cannot be traced back to any record.

**Round 2 — `partial_histogram`.** Each node places its records into that grid
and returns *record counts per bin*, pooled across groups. The central server
sums those counts and merges adjacent bins into roughly equal-count **rank
bins** — narrow where the data is dense, wide where it is sparse — then computes
each rank bin's global mid-rank.

**Round 3 — `partial_rank_sums`.** Each node assigns its records the mid-rank of
the bin they fall into and returns, per (column, group), three numbers: the sum
of those scores, their sum of squares, and the record count. The test statistic
follows from those alone. No per-group distribution is transmitted at all.

So the complete payload leaving a node is a handful of counts, sums and sums of
squares. No value, no rank of any individual record, no identifier, no ordering.

### Disclosure control

Aggregates can still be revealing when they describe very few records, so three
thresholds are enforced at the node before anything is returned:

| Setting | Default | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MINIMUM_NUMBER_OF_RECORDS` | 10 | A node holding this many records or fewer refuses to contribute at all. |
| `BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MINIMUM_GROUP_SIZE` | 5 | A (column, group) cell smaller than this is dropped from every round and reported as suppressed. |
| `BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MINIMUM_CELL_COUNT` | 5 | No individual histogram cell below this count is ever transmitted. |

The last one matters most: on a fine grid, a bin count of `1` would pin a record
down to a narrow interval and effectively leak its value. Before the histogram
leaves the node, adjacent bins are accumulated until the running total reaches
the threshold and that total is reported at the run's count-weighted centre, so
every non-zero cell describes at least five records while the record count and
the shape of the distribution are preserved. The central server additionally
requires every rank bin to hold at least `MINIMUM_CELL_COUNT` records per
contributing node, so the bins that ranking is based on are non-disclosive too.
The number of rank bins used is returned under `privacy.n_rank_bins_per_column`,
together with the number of suppressed cells.

### Accuracy of the approximation

Records that land in the same rank bin are treated as tied. Both statistics are
computed in their **general-scores** form — `H = (N-1) × SSB / SST` for
Kruskal-Wallis, and a standardised linear rank statistic for Mann-Whitney. Fed
exact mid-ranks these are algebraically identical to the textbook tie-corrected
formulas, which `test/test_math.py` verifies against `scipy.stats.kruskal` and
`scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu` to floating-point precision, with and without heavy
ties.

This form is used rather than the classical `12/(N(N+1)) · Σ R²/n − 3(N+1)`
because it only assumes the scores are a monotone function of the values, not
that they are a perfect permutation of `1..N`. The binning protocol produces
scores that drift a fraction of a percent off that ideal; the classical formula
is a difference of two large, nearly equal terms, and it turns that drift into a
wildly wrong statistic — including false positives on null columns. The
general-scores form is insensitive to it.

What remains is the binning itself. On the bundled test data the federated
statistic lands within a few percent of the exact value, and the test suite
checks that the result is stable whether the data is split across 1, 2, 5 or 10
nodes. The correction itself is exact — the ranking of hypotheses is driven by
p-values, so small perturbations only matter for hypotheses sitting right on the
threshold.

If you need the exact statistic, run the dedicated `v6-kruskal-wallis-py` or
`v6-mann-whitney-u-test-py` algorithm and feed its p-values to `central`. Note
that those algorithms transmit the raw values to the central server in their
first round, which is the trade-off this algorithm is designed to avoid.

## Configuration

The thresholds above are read with `get_env_var`, so a node administrator can
tighten them per node without changing the image:

```yaml
algorithm_env:
  BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MINIMUM_NUMBER_OF_RECORDS: 25
  BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MINIMUM_GROUP_SIZE: 10
  BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MINIMUM_CELL_COUNT: 10
```

`BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_HISTOGRAM_BINS` (default 200),
`BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_MAXIMUM_RANK_BINS` (default 50) and
`BENJAMINI_HOCHBERG_RANGE_SD` (default 4.0) control the reporting grid
resolution, the cap on the number of rank bins, and the span of the grid.

## Testing

```bash
# statistics only — needs numpy and scipy, not vantage6
python test/test_math.py

# end-to-end against the vantage6 mock client
python test/test.py
```

## Dockerizing your algorithm

To finally run your algorithm on the vantage6 infrastructure, you need to
create a Docker image of your algorithm.

A Docker image can be created by executing the following command in the root of your
algorithm directory:

```bash
docker build -t [my_docker_image_name] .
```

where you should provide a sensible value for the Docker image name. The
`docker build` command will create a Docker image that contains your algorithm.
You can create an additional tag for it by running

```bash
docker tag [my_docker_image_name] [another_image_name]
```

This way, you can e.g. do
`docker tag local_benjamini_hochberg_algorithm harbor2.vantage6.ai/algorithms/benjamini-hochberg` to
make the algorithm available on a remote Docker registry (in this case
`harbor2.vantage6.ai`).

Finally, you need to push the image to the Docker registry. This can be done
by running

```bash
docker push [my_docker_image_name]
```

Note that you need to be logged in to the Docker registry before you can push
the image. You can do this by running `docker login` and providing your
credentials. Check [this page](https://docs.docker.com/get-started/04_sharing_app/)
For more details on sharing images on Docker Hub. If you are using a different
Docker registry, check the documentation of that registry and be sure that you
have sufficient permissions.
